WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.940
Michael B. Jordan gained 24 pounds of pure&nbsp;
muscle in under a year. For us normal people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.902
that can sound like sorcery, but the real magic&nbsp;
is the Hollywood health plan he was following.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.580
Take a look at Jordan's Instagram&nbsp;
pictures before his training intensified,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.660
and you'll see that the actor has&nbsp;
never really been out of shape. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.200
it's a far cry from the shredded&nbsp;
frame he's had in more recent movies.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.300
Everything changed with his casting as Adonis&nbsp;
Creed. Playing the son of "Rocky" opponent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.220
Apollo Creed required him to look the part of&nbsp;
a boxer in peak condition. Even though Jordan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.300
describes himself as a competitive athlete,&nbsp;
there's a different level of fitness required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.340
for a boxer. According to trainer Corey&nbsp;
Calliet, it was hard-going at first, saying,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.920
"When we first started, he&nbsp;
could barely bench 20 pounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.940
his legs were flailing everywhere ... he&nbsp;
had nothing. He used to hate all this."

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.000
He might have hated the training,&nbsp;
but he can't hate the results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.635
since he put on 24 pounds&nbsp;
of muscle in just a year.

00:00:54.635 --> 00:00:58.200
There's no easy shortcut for putting&nbsp;
on so much muscle in such a short time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.120
but a Hollywood trainer makes it a little&nbsp;
more manageable. Enter Calliet, a former boxer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.320
whose experience was instrumental in crafting&nbsp;
Adonis Creed's physique. According to Jordan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.880
Calliet was the first trainer that he clicked&nbsp;
with after a series of false starts with others.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.180
Calliet stayed with Jordan when he&nbsp;
got into a different type of fitness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.360
for his role as Erik Killmonger&nbsp;
for the original "Black Panther."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.740
Calliet played both sides of the movie's&nbsp;
conflict though. He also worked to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.420
Angela Bassett in fighting shape. Bassett&nbsp;
played Ramonda, the matriarch of Wakanda.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.560
According to Calliet, whether he's training&nbsp;
someone in their twenties like Jordan or in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.640
their fifties like Bassett, the technique&nbsp;
is the same. Focusing on high-intensity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.046
interval training is the best way to burn&nbsp;
fat and highlight certain muscle groups.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.360
To properly portray a boxer, Jordan had&nbsp;
to train like a boxer. That meant 45-60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.040
minutes of cardio daily, along with tons&nbsp;
of ab workouts. As for building muscle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.620
Calliet had Jordan focus on supersets — exercises&nbsp;
where lifting or curling dumbbells are mixed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.060
push-ups or another workout. In addition,&nbsp;
Calliet pushed Jordan with circuit training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.404
building his endurance with quick&nbsp;
rounds of different cardio exercises.

00:02:03.478 --> 00:02:07.038
"Yeah, that's cool, but you know, maybe&nbsp;
you could just give me some drills."

00:02:08.529 --> 00:02:09.050
"Drills?"

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:10.140
"Yeah, you know, things I could do on my own."

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.500
If that all seems exhausting, we&nbsp;
haven't even gotten to the main event:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.780
a full three hours of boxing training following&nbsp;
every workout day. In talking about the workout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.600
Calliet had this to say in an&nbsp;
interview for "Men's Journal":

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.039
"I wanted to make him look even more ripped&nbsp;
than Apollo, and I think we did that."

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.880
According to Jordan, his lifestyle&nbsp;
changed completely once he began to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.120
see results from working out for&nbsp;
roles. About the change, he said,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.080
"When things start getting a little easier, when&nbsp;
you start lifting weight you never lifted before…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.160
Then it's, 'This is all right.' So I learned&nbsp;
to love it when I started seeing results."

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.480
Being in fighting shape from "Creed" helped&nbsp;
Jordan transition to the training for "Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.320
Panther," where he used a new technique&nbsp;
called "Muscle Training." The technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.860
adapted Jordan's workouts to match what&nbsp;
the character would do to stay in shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:56.160
which helped alleviate the&nbsp;
dreaded workout plateau.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.420
The first "Creed" established that Adonis was&nbsp;
fighting in the Light Heavyweight division,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:06.720
which in boxing has an upper limit of 175&nbsp;
pounds. For the 2018 sequel "Creed II," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.120
Jordan had to bulk up in order to fight Viktor&nbsp;
Drago, the fearsome son of Ivan Drago, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.520
by Florian Munteanu. For context, Ivan killed&nbsp;
Adonis' father in the ring three decades earlier.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.800
The film gives Jordan an unconventional training&nbsp;
regimen. After losing to Drago in their first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.720
bout, Adonis rebuilds himself out in the desert&nbsp;
alongside Rocky and his trainer. In real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.080
however, Jordan and Calliet did&nbsp;
things a bit more by the book.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.120
For the 2021 Amazon film "Without Remorse,"&nbsp;
based on the Tom Clancy novel, Jordan had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.740
develop a more holistic workout plan that even&nbsp;
involved tactical training. The actor was cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.600
as John Kelly, an elite Navy SEAL whose wife&nbsp;
and family are murdered in a home invasion.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.960
As with "Black Panther," military&nbsp;
training was essential to the character;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.860
Jordan can't just look good, he has to be&nbsp;
able to move and fight with efficiency.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:00.780
His training regimen included a 12-week course&nbsp;
in weapons training, similar to what real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.280
soldiers would receive. Calliet was also concerned&nbsp;
with some aspects of Jordan's physique that don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.480
get as much attention when he's in the&nbsp;
ring — namely, his legs and cardio. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.860
two employed a CrossFit-style program utilizing&nbsp;
high-intensity interval training, in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.180
to the ten hours a day Jordan would spend in&nbsp;
front of the camera and in weapons training.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.680
Playing larger-than-life characters&nbsp;
can be a double-edged sword. Once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.920
you've hit those heights, it's hard&nbsp;
to come back to earth. After five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.400
years straight of playing nothing but&nbsp;
comic book characters, champion boxers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.000
and elite soldiers, it was jarring to&nbsp;
see him play a mere mortal once again.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.280
In 2019, Jordan starred in the death row&nbsp;
drama "Just Mercy." The film cast him as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.560
celebrated civil rights attorney Bryan Stevenson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.752
working to free an innocent man&nbsp;
awaiting execution in 1980s Alabama.

00:04:44.752 --> 00:04:51.135
"If we can look at ourselves closely, we&nbsp;
can change this world for the better."

00:04:51.135 --> 00:04:54.600
Jordan has a lean figure in his&nbsp;
portrayal of young Stevenson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.200
dressed in sharp suits that are perhaps&nbsp;
a little too fitted and not boxy enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:03.120
to really be from the late '80s. One person less&nbsp;
than thrilled at Jordan's toned-down silhouette,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.200
however, was the real-life Stevenson.&nbsp;
He joked in 2020 when told Jordan,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.800
"'The one area where we don't have to&nbsp;
be authentic is that you don't have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.670
lose that 'Black Panther,' 'Creed' body&nbsp;
when you play me. You can keep that.'"

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.320
"Creed III" arrived in theaters nearly five&nbsp;
years after "Creed II," with its star in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.580
new phase of his life and now with new&nbsp;
challenges to face as well. For Adonis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.820
the challenge comes in the form of&nbsp;
a ghost from the past. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.540
he has to fight a childhood friend who&nbsp;
spent decades in prison and now wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:35.760
a shot at the title from the now-retired champ.&nbsp;
For Jordan, the film marks his directorial debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.080
taking the reins from previous franchise&nbsp;
leaders Ryan Coogler and Steven Caple, Jr.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.880
Behind the scenes, the film proved to be a new&nbsp;
challenge for Calliet as well. He had to plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.320
a workout regimen that could be done while still&nbsp;
allowing Jordan to fulfill his duties as not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.940
the film's star, but also its director. Training&nbsp;
began four months out from the start of filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:58.320
with Calliet putting his star through the&nbsp;
regimen up to three hours a day, six days a week.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.940
After filming began, however, workouts became&nbsp;
less regular due to the demands of Jordan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:07.260
schedule. He and Calliet would do half-hour&nbsp;
bursts of strength training, calisthenics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:11.880
or the same kind of high-intensity interval&nbsp;
training they had developed for previous films.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.260
Calliet told Insider that his goal for "Creed&nbsp;
III" was to get Jordan into the best shape he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:20.820
had ever been for the series. For Jordan, there&nbsp;
were other concerns beyond just getting swole.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:25.020
The body of Adonis tells a story in the&nbsp;
"Creed" films, just as Stallone transforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:30.540
from an unremarkable heavyweight in "Rocky" to&nbsp;
a superhumanly-toned warrior in "Rocky IV". At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:34.500
this point, Adonis is comfortably retired,&nbsp;
living off the fat of his accomplishments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.820
the ring and a new generation of fighters&nbsp;
he is promoting. In fact, Jordan went so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:43.740
far as to describe his physique in a 2023 GQ&nbsp;
interview as similar to a typical dad's body.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.240
While those who rock the dad bod in real life&nbsp;
might scoff at the movie star using their phrase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.380
his statement provides a good insight into&nbsp;
how Jordan views Adonis at this point in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.400
the franchise. It may also be telling about&nbsp;
how he views himself compared to his co-star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.660
Jonathon Majors, who also trained with&nbsp;
Calliet. Adonis, like Rocky before him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:05.220
always has to be the underdog going into&nbsp;
each film's climactic bout. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.321
a dad bod is the perfect&nbsp;
obstacle to have to overcome.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.000
Much of the coverage around Jordan's&nbsp;
transformation over the years has focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.380
on his workouts and the way different roles&nbsp;
require a different focus. An important part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.000
of his fitness plan that is often overlooked&nbsp;
was his diet. For Jordan and Calliet on "Creed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.840
III," the focus was on lean proteins and&nbsp;
whole foods like rice and vegetables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.700
with fruit and protein shakes as a snack. While&nbsp;
a diet of turkey and rice might seem extreme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:34.440
Calliet is also a firm believer in cheat meals,&nbsp;
which would actually help fill out his muscles.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.640
In their joint interview with GQ, Majors&nbsp;
emphasized how important a steady diet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.760
is to maintaining such impossible&nbsp;
physiques, claiming to eat about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.260
4,000 calories a day when preparing for&nbsp;
the role. He also emphasized an even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.760
overlooked aspect of fitness: A healthy&nbsp;
sleep schedule. About sleep, he said,

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:54.780
"Sleep hygiene is, like, everything.&nbsp;
You've [Jordan] got great skin, man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:59.495
and if you don't sleep your skin won't be&nbsp;
like that. Your muscles won't grow like that."

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:03.180
There is something about playing a boxer&nbsp;
on screen that seems to convince an actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.240
they could do it in real life. Maybe&nbsp;
it's the months of training required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.900
to look the part of a boxer that makes them&nbsp;
want to take the leap from fiction to fact.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.720
While Jordan has never said&nbsp;
he could be a real fighter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.260
or even that he wants to, one of&nbsp;
his compatriots has done it for him:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:21.120
Former cruiserweight Tony Bellew, who&nbsp;
played opponent "Pretty" Ricky Conlan.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.860
Bellew, who famously floored Jordan by accident&nbsp;
while filming the climax of the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.420
was impressed with the actor's athletic&nbsp;
prowess and work ethic. In a March 2023&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:34.260
interview with Virgin Radio, he claimed&nbsp;
Jordan could be a real thing, saying,

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:37.000
"He would eat a couple of&nbsp;
these YouTubers, no problem."

